In Java, class (lowercase) and Class (uppercase) refer to two very different concepts:

🔹 1. class (lowercase) — Keyword
This is a reserved keyword used to define a class in Java.

It tells the compiler you're about to declare a class.

✅ Example:

public class Car {
    String model;
}
In this example, class is a keyword used to define the class named Car.

🔹 2. Class (uppercase) — Class in java.lang package
Class is a built-in final class in java.lang.

It represents the metadata of a Java class (i.e., runtime representation).

It’s part of Java Reflection API and allows you to inspect or manipulate class details at runtime.

✅ Example:

public class Car {
    String model;
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car car = new Car();

        // Getting class metadata using getClass()
        Class<?> clazz = car.getClass();

        System.out.println("Class Name: " + clazz.getName());
    }
}
Output:


Class Name: Car
🔍 Key Differences:
| Feature | `class`             | `Class`                                        |
| ------- | ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Type    | Keyword             | Java class (`java.lang.Class`)                 |
| Purpose | Defines a new class | Represents the structure of a class at runtime |
| Usage   | `class MyClass {}`  | `Class<?> clazz = obj.getClass();`             |
| Used in | Code declaration    | Reflection & metadata inspection               |


